// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ARC 1 — TRUST-LAW RECONCILIATION  (code first, then paper)
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

arc_1_trust_reconciliation:

  status: "CLOSED — session 2026-06-16e. Law A (cos-driven + soplo) canonical.
           Ported to run_s1_kernel_loop + run_spec_boot. spec_boot regression PASSED
           (D_eff=6.00, stasis_cos≈0.95 ∈ Ω_A). EXP-L1-01 re-certified: trust=[1,1,1,1],
           H1/H2/H5 PASS, H4 marginal (calibration artifact — cos=0.95 > θ_A=0.88 real bound).
           Verdict: viability-equivalent but not μ*-equivalent (shared Ω_A, V>0; attractor
           location differs: cos→1 under B vs cos≈0.95 under A).
           §Discussion §sec:trustlaw written (5 paragraphs + TikZ fig:trustlaw_geom).
           Committed: feat(nucleus+paper): reconcile trust law → law A canonical; close ARC 1
           G-F now unblocked."

  the_two_laws:
    law_A_cos_driven:
      formula:   "trust_drift = 0.025·cos − 0.04·(1−cos) = 0.065·cos − 0.04 ;
                  PLUS soplo reset: trust += 0.18 when trust < 0.75"
      validated: "EXP-CORE-01 (docs/experiments/EXP-CORE-01/results.vsm)"
      behaviour: "globally stable in trust-space — recovers to cos=1.0 even from trust=0"
      code:      "run_core_calibration (src/main.rs ~line 1090–1160; soplo-reset path)"
    law_B_phase_driven:
      formula:   "trust += 0.002 in stasis ; trust −= 0.001 in degen ; NO reset"
      runs_in:   "run_s1_kernel_loop (src/main.rs line 2867/2869) AND
                  run_spec_boot (src/main.rs line 1821/1834)"
      consumers: "cgroup_multikernel, multikernel_geometry, s3_failure_resilience,
                  recursive_thermal_ramp, real_process_governance (EXP-L1-01), spec_boot (EXP-SB-01)"
      behaviour: "drains to 0 under a degen-dominated regime (what EXP-L1-01 hit in pure compute)"

  the_tension:
    "At cos=0.897 (EXP-L1-01's stall point): law A gives drift = 0.065·0.897 − 0.04 = +0.018 > 0
     → trust GROWS. Law B drained it to 0. So the collapse is purely the law-B regime, not physics.
     Yet EXP-SB-01 (the paper's anchor convergence experiment) ALSO uses law B and DOES converge —
     because at short horizons it stays stasis-dominated. The divergence only bites at long horizons
     / pure-compute regimes. This is the model≡reality crack: the paper's trust-stability claim
     (law A, EXP-CORE-01) is not the law that runs in the multikernel / vOS Stage 1 (law B)."

  reconciliation_method:  // this IS the paper-worthy contribution (see paper_material below)
    framing: "Two operators f₀ that share a fixed point x̄ and local basin but differ in (i) global
              contraction rate and (ii) off-basin behaviour. The Lyapunov framework already owns the
              language: recurrent set R, stationary measure μ*, and Cor_ReframeRecertification
              (docs/theory/lyapunov_proof.vsm). The general question:
                'When do two dynamical laws induce the SAME viable system?'
              Answer/criterion: when they share R, μ*, and Ω_A. That is a viability-equivalence
              criterion — the reusable principle, not the incident."
    steps: [
      "S1. Read BOTH loops fully: run_core_calibration (law A) + run_s1_kernel_loop (law B). Confirm
           the exact update rules + where soplo-reset lives in A and is absent in B.",
      "S2. Decide the CANONICAL law. Default recommendation: law A (cos-driven + soplo-reset) — it is
           the one the paper validates and the one with proven global recovery. Confirm with rmw3.",
      "S3. Port the canonical law into run_s1_kernel_loop (and reconcile spec_boot). Keep the change
           minimal + documented; this touches EXP-SB-01's anchor loop, so re-run spec_boot and verify
           stasis_cos 0.8055→1.000 still reproduces (regression guard on the paper's key result).",
      "S4. Re-run EXP-L1-01 Phase A under the canonical law on LXC 101. Expectation: trust recovers,
           H4 (stasis_cos>0.95) passes. Record in docs/experiments/index.vsm phase_a_results.",
      "S5. Compute R, μ*, Ω_A for both laws (or argue analytically) → show whether they are
           viability-equivalent or genuinely different systems. This is the evidence the paper needs.",
    ]
    gate: "S5 result decides the paper framing. If laws ARE viability-equivalent → 'robustness of the
           viability claim under law choice'. If NOT → 'law choice matters; here is the canonical one
           and why' (stronger model≡reality story). Either way the arc must be CLOSED before writing."

  paper_material:
    verdict: "YES — include, but ONLY after the arc is closed (S1–S5 done). NEVER publish the open arc."
    where:   "§Discussion, new subsection, linked to Cor_ReframeRecertification."
    framing: "NOT 'we had two contradictory laws (a bug)'. Frame as: 'viability is invariant under a
              class of equivalent dynamical laws; here is the criterion (shared R, μ*, Ω_A) and its
              verification.' The incident is the worked example, not the thesis."
    risk:    "Reviewer (Fierro) could read an open/ill-framed version as 'which loop produced which
              empirical result?' — undermining trust in ALL results. The closed+principled framing
              is what blocks that. fig G-F (ARC 2) is its visual."
    continuity: "Extends existing fig6_trust_bifurcation — natural visual lineage."


